{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>A half shekel of the shekel of the sanctuary.</b> Our master Moses established a silver coin among Israel, for he was a great king, and he called that coin \"shekel\" because the entire coin was of full weight, with no deficiency and no dross silver. And because the valuations (Leviticus 27:25) and the redemption of the firstborn (Numbers 18:16) were paid with that coin, which are sacred, and likewise all the shekels of the Tabernacle (Exodus 38:24) and likewise every fixed amount of silver mentioned in the Torah (Deuteronomy 22:29), Scripture calls it \"the shekel of the sanctuary.\" And similarly the reason seems to me in what our Sages call the language of the Torah \"the sacred language\" — this is because the words of the Torah and the Prophets and all words of holiness were all spoken in that language. And behold, it is the language in which HaShem, exalted be His name, speaks with His prophets and with His congregation — \"I am\" and \"You shall not have\" and the rest of the commandments of the Torah and prophecy — and in it He is called by His sacred names: El, Elohim, Tzevaot, Shaddai, and the Name with yod-he, and the great Name that is uniquely One, and in it He created His world (Genesis Rabbah 18:6), and He called the names of heaven and earth and all that is in them, and His angels and all His hosts — all of them He called by name: Michael and Gabriel in that language, and in it He called names for the holy ones who are on earth — Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, Solomon, and the rest.\n\nAnd the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:8): Do not think that our language is called the sacred language out of our pride or our error, but it is fitting, for this language is holy — you will not find in it names for the organ of copulation in the male or the female, nor for semen and urine and excrement, except by euphemism. And let not \"shegal\" (Psalms 45:10) mislead you, for it is a name for a woman designated for lying down, and Scripture says \"yishgalennah\" (Deuteronomy 28:30) according to what was written upon it, and its meaning is: he will take a woman as a concubine. But there is no need for this explanation, for the matter is clear: the sacred language is holy as I have explained, and the reason he gave is, in my opinion, not correct, for the fact that they used the euphemism \"yishgalennah\" for \"yishkavennah\" (he will lie with her) indicates that \"mishgal\" is an actual term for copulation, and likewise they used the euphemism \"to eat their holes\" (II Kings 18:27), for it is a disgraceful term. And if the Rav's reason were the true one, they would have called it \"clean language,\" in the sense of what we learned (Sanhedrin 68b) — that the lower beard goes round and not the upper — except that the Sages spoke in clean language, and they said \"but the bread which he eats\" as clean language (Genesis Rabbah 86:7), and similarly in many places.\n\nAnd Scripture explained that the shekel is twenty silver gerah, and Onkelos translated (Onkelos on Exodus 30:13) \"ma'in,\" for gerah with him is a name for the coin called in Aramaic \"ma'ah,\" and likewise Jonathan ben Uziel translated (I Samuel 2:36) \"for a silver agorah\" — \"for a silver ma'ah,\" and likewise he rendered the shekel of the sanctuary as \"sela,\" for that is the name of the coin in Aramaic, and its measure is also known in the Talmud (Kiddushin 11a). And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Exodus 21:32) that the shekel weighs four gold pieces, which is half an ounce by the straight weight of Cologne. The Rav found it explicitly stated in the Gemara (Bava Metzia 35b) that the sela is four dinars, and he accepted the weight of the dinar as equivalent to the weight of the gold pieces. And so he wrote in his commentary on the Gemara in Bava Kamma (36b, s.v. kesef tzuri): the dinar weighs one gold piece, and likewise in Constantinople they call the gold piece a dinar. All of this is true, but the Rav estimated using the gold pieces found in his generation and also in our generation, and they weigh as much as the half-ounce he mentioned — but this is not so, for the kings of the nations diminished the gold pieces. And we have already found in the words of the author of the Halakhot Gedolot (Hilchot Bekhorot and Hilchot Kiddushin) and the early Geonim that the dinar mentioned everywhere in the Talmud is the shashdang dinar, and so it is written in tractate Kiddushin in the laws of our Rav (the Rif, chapter 1), and he said that it is the gold dinar of the Arabs. And according to the measures found in their words, the dinars of the Talmud are larger than the gold pieces current in our time — close to a third of a shekel — and the shekel is three-quarters of an ounce by the weight of this land, and this is the ounce that the Rav, of blessed memory, mentioned.\n\nAnd know that the shekels of the Torah are these selaim, each sela being four dinars, but the shekel mentioned in the words of the Sages — such as what we learned (Shevuot 43a): \"a shekel I lent you, and a sela it was worth; a sela I lent you, and a shekel it was worth\" — is two dinars, half a sela. The reason for this is that the people call the half-selaim that they weighed every year \"shekels,\" and this became the usage in the language of the Sages in the Mishnah. Therefore a person says to his fellow, \"A shekel I lent you,\" meaning the weight that Israel weighs. And it is possible that in the time of the Second Temple they made a silver coin of two dinars so that it would be readily available to give to the treasurer of the sanctuary and they would not need to make change, and they called this coin \"shekel,\" and they called the shekel of Moses — which is the shekel of the Torah — \"sela,\" as in its Aramaic translation. And there are those who say that the reason is from what they said (Bekhorot 50a) that the sacred maneh was doubled, and so too all the coins. But this is not correct, for the shekels of a slave, of rape, and of seduction are not sacred."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "In the citation from Sanhedrin, the printed editions read 68a; Ramban cites 68b. The passage about the lower beard round and not the upper refers to a Talmudic euphemism."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:19:00.920020644Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}